	issn = {0956-5000},
	doi = {10.1039/ft9908601167},
	abstract = {We review the types of atomic migration mechanism operative in ionic solids with exceptionally high ionic mobilities. We classify mechanisms as simple-hopping, highly correlated or liquid-like. We discuss current knowledge of transport mechanisms in fluorite structured ionic conductors, /3-Al2O,, Agl and proton conductors. Fast-ion conductors are solids with exceptionally high ionic conductivities, typically ca. 0.01-10 0-' cm-'. The class of materials is extraordinarily diverse and includes simple inor-ganic compounds such as PbF, and AgI, and organic poly-mers such as polyethylene oxide (PEO) doped with metal salts. They have attracted considerable attention particularly during the last 15 years, owing to both their considerable potential in several technologies, e.g. high-energy density battery and sensor development, and the fundamental scien-tific interest in the phenomenon of high ionic mobility in solids. An overview of the field can be obtained from the several conference and reviews7?* that have been published in recent years. This article will focus on the state of our present knowledge of the mechanisms of ionic transport in these materials. We shall see that despite the large quantity of literature on fast-ion conduction, fundamen-tal mechanistic features are still unknown or uncertain in some of the most important materials. 1. Fast-ion Conductors : Classification and Techniques 1.1 Types of Material We have already noted the diversity of materials showing high ionic mobility. The most important classes of fast-ion conducting solids are as follows. (i) Solids with Phase Transitions. Those compounds for which the higher-temperature phase shows fast-ion behaviour are of interest. The transitions may be first order as in AgI for example or diffuse, as in SrCl, and PbF, (and apparently, all fluorite structured compounds). (ii) Layer-and Tunnel-structured Compounds. For these com-pounds ion transport is confined to two or one dimensions, respectively. Of the many members of this class we give as examples first the celebrated B-and p-Al,O, compounds (see fig. 1) in which the mobile cations are located in conduc-tion planes between the spinel-structured alumina blocks, secondly the intriguing lithium conductor, Li,N, which will be discussed subsequently and whose unique crystal structure is illustrated in fig. 2. The widely studied NASICON com-pound (Na,Zr,PSi,O,,) provides an example of a three-dimensional tunnel structure. (iii) Heavily Doped and Massively Disordered Solids. High concentrations of mobile defects are created by the intro-duction of high dopant concentrations, as in the doped fluo-rite oxides, e.g. Y doped CeO, where the mobile defects are oxygen vacancies compensating the Y},
	number = {8},
	urldate = {2017-01-18},
	journal = {J. CHEM. SOC. FARADAY TRANS},
	author = {Richard, C and Catlow, A},
	year = {1990},
	note = {ISBN: 0956-5000},
	pages = {1167--1176},
}

